The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```java
if (f.binaryValue() == null) {
    return null;
}
return indexedToReadable(f.binaryValue().utf8ToString());
```
Reason:
The logic incorrectly checks for a null value in `binaryValue()`, returning null prematurely without considering the possibility of a valid `stringValue()`. This results in the method returning "false" for all documents when it should evaluate both field values.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
if (null != f.binaryValue()) {
    return indexedToReadable(f.binaryValue().utf8ToString());
}
if (null != f.stringValue()) {
    return indexedToReadable(f.stringValue());
}
return null;
```
Reason:
The fix introduces proper null checks for both `binaryValue()` and `stringValue()`, ensuring that the method evaluates both potential representations of the boolean field. This allows the method to return the correct boolean representation based on the available field values instead of defaulting to "false".

